You are performing a one-time meta-audit of the Harn release and bump-fleet
harnesses. This audit is intentionally outside the normal release workflow and
outside CI. Treat it as a disposable but high-signal investigation whose output
can become issues or follow-up PRs across harn-bump-fleet and Harn.

Use the deterministic facts first, then inspect implementation files with the
read-only tools. Do not mutate files, push branches, dispatch workflows, publish
releases, or recommend adding this audit to the main release/bump loops.

Look for concrete friction a local model would experience:
- confusing or contradictory instructions,
- missing context that caused loops, retries, or wrong assumptions,
- brittle format checks, hidden deterministic failures, or unhelpful artifacts,
- places where Harn stdlib, connector, transcript, tool-search, or agent-loop
  abstractions should absorb harness-specific code,
- places where the harness should stay simple and not generalize.

Prefer evidence over speculation. If you identify a problem, cite the artifact
path, transcript symptom, source file, or prompt that supports it. When the
deterministic facts already answer something, do not spend tool budget
rediscovering it.

Required final shape:

# Harness Self-Review

## Executive Summary

Short bullets with the highest-value takeaways.

## Findings

For each finding, include severity, evidence, why it mattered, and a concrete
ticket-sized recommendation. Keep findings ordered by practical impact.

## What Worked

Note harness or Harn behavior that was healthy and should not be overfixed.

## Cross-Repo Opportunities

Separate harn-bump-fleet follow-ups from Harn stdlib/runtime/tooling follow-ups.

## Non-Issues / Avoid

Call out tempting changes that are not worth doing.

Keep the report concise but specific enough that another session can file or
implement the action items.

Your response must be only the final report. The first line must be exactly
`# Harness Self-Review`. Do not include scratchpad notes, planning text,
"key observations", draft bullets, or commentary about how you are constructing
the report.
